├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ double_smoothed_zero_lag_filter                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the exponentially-weighted moving average with a zero-lag double-smoothing filter │
│                          │ (DSSA / "zero-lag EMA" style error-correction). A single EMA introduces phase lag         │
│                          │ proportional to window length, hurting lag_error and responsiveness. The zero-lag         │
│                          │ technique runs two passes of EMA and adds back the estimated lag error (error = input -   │
│                          │ EMA of EMA, corrected output = 2*EMA1 - EMA2), which cancels most of the group delay      │
│                          │ while retaining strong noise attenuation. This simultaneously reduces lag_error (error    │
│                          │ correction term), slope changes and false reversals (smoother trend), while improving     │
│                          │ correlation since output tracks the clean signal phase-aligned. To keep output length     │
│                          │ consistent with the current API, the filter is applied on the full signal and the last    │
│                          │ output_length values are returned, aligned to the window end as before.                   │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
