│ patch_name               │ adaptive_rts_smoother_trend_filter                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ Instead of a one-pass causal Kalman filter followed by Savitzky-Golay passes, this        │
│                          │ approach uses a fundamentally different strategy: (1) noise-adaptive                      │
│                          │ constant-acceleration Kalman filter run forward, (2) a Rauch-Tung-Striebel (RTS) backward │
│                          │ smoothing pass that yields the optimal minimum-variance state estimate over the whole     │
│                          │ record (dramatically reducing lag error and false reversals since the smoother uses       │
│                          │ future context optimally), and (3) a trend-stability refinement that suppresses spurious  │
│                          │ slope sign flips by locally projecting the slope estimate onto piecewise-consistent       │
│                          │ trends. The adaptive process/measurement noise ratio is estimated from robust local       │
│                          │ statistics (median absolute deviation of first and second differences), so the filter     │
│                          │ automatically tunes smoothing strength to volatility. Because RTS smoothing is zero-phase │
│                          │ and optimal in the least-squares sense, it should simultaneously improve lag error, slope │
│                          │ changes, false reversals, tracking accuracy and smoothness compared to the heuristic      │
│                          │ cascade.                                                                                  │
