│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ rts_smoother_over_forward_kalman                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ The current causal Kalman filter scores well on responsiveness (0.85) and accuracy (0.91) │
│                          │ but suffers from high slope changes (317) and false reversals (254) — classic symptoms of │
│                          │ a filter that is too responsive relative to its noise floor. The optimal fix under the    │
│                          │ constant-acceleration model is a Rauch-Tung-Striebel (RTS) smoother: it reuses the        │
│                          │ already-computed forward pass but runs the standard backward recursion `s = s_f + C_i (s  │
│                          │ - s_pred)` with `C_i = P_f F' P_pred^{-1}`. This gives the minimum-variance estimate at   │
│                          │ each time given ALL samples, cutting spurious slope reversals dramatically while keeping  │
│                          │ the same model/noise estimates, hence no extra lag relative to the aligned output.        │
│                          │ Crucially, to avoid the length/alignment bugs that killed prior attempts: I store         │
│                          │ per-step filtered states and covariances during the forward loop, run RTS through that    │
│                          │ sequence, then trim with `smoothed` so the output length is exactly `n - window_size + 1` │
│                          │ and end-aligned with the input (the smoothest, best-estimated region). The trend-reversal │
│                          │ gate and Huber outlier guard are retained in the forward pass.                            │
│ num_applied              │ 3                                                                                         │
