│ patch_name               │ fb_kalman_sg_smoother                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ This approach replaces the laggy sliding-window average with a fundamentally different    │
│                          │ pipeline: (1) a robust noise estimate from first differences (MAD-based, insensitive to   │
│                          │ outliers), (2) a forward–backward (two-pass) constant-velocity Kalman smoother — running  │
│                          │ the filter forward then backward and optimally blending the two runs gives                │
│                          │ near-RTS-smoother quality with zero phase distortion, dramatically improving noise        │
│                          │ reduction and lag, and (3) a zero-phase Savitzky-Golay (local quadratic fit) smoothing    │
│                          │ pass with reflected-boundary padding to eliminate spurious slope reversals without adding │
│                          │ delay. This combination directly targets the four objectives: slope-change/false-reversal │
│                          │ counts collapse due to the polynomial smoothing stage, lag error is minimized by the      │
│                          │ zero-phase design and the trend (velocity) state, tracking accuracy is maintained by      │
│                          │ adaptive process noise that boosts gain when innovations grow, and computational cost     │
│                          │ remains O(n).                                                                             │
