│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ rtss_slope_comp_sg_polish                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ Two targeted improvements on top of the working Kalman+RTS core:                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1) **Lag/lead compensation using the smoothed slope state.** The RTS smoother gives us    │
│                          │ not just a level estimate but a slope estimate `smoothed[:,1]` that is already optimally  │
│                          │ denoised. The remaining lag_error (0.26) and avg_error (0.29) come from the filter's      │
│                          │ effective group delay. Shifting each output forward by a fraction of `window_size` times  │
│                          │ the smoothed slope (a first-order Taylor lead) recovers responsiveness without adding     │
│                          │ noise, since the slope channel is heavily smoothed by both the Kalman model and the       │
│                          │ backward pass.                                                                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2) **Light Savitzky-Golay polish.** slope_changes (82) and false_reversals (60) are still │
│                          │ the weakest metrics. A short (length 7, order 2) Savitzky-Golay pass over the             │
│                          │ already-smoothed estimates preserves quadratic trends exactly (so genuine turning points  │
│                          │ are untouched, order 2 reproduces local curvature) while averaging out residual           │
│                          │ high-frequency ripple in the level channel. The short window keeps added phase delay far  │
│                          │ below the window-size scale, and the end-aligned trim is computed after both stages so    │
│                          │ the output contract (length = n - window_size + 1, end-aligned) is rigorously preserved.  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ These two operations act on different artifacts (lag from delay, ripple-driven slope      │
│                          │ reversals), so their gains compound while neither re-introduces the other's problem.      │
