├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ denoised_kalman_slope_lead_compensation                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ The current program (combined_score 0.59) already pairs a causal constant-velocity Kalman │
│                          │ filter with a zero-phase Savitzky-Golay pass, which is why it leads on smoothness         │
│                          │ (slope_changes 107, false_reversals 83) and correlation (0.95). Its remaining weakness is │
│                          │ the residual group delay of the causal Kalman stage: lag_error 0.29, responsiveness 0.77, │
│                          │ noise_reduction 0.69. The evaluation feedback recommends compensating this lag with a     │
│                          │ trend lead of ~0.4× the SG half-window, but stresses that the slope feeding the lead must │
│                          │ come from a *denoised* estimate — a raw `np.gradient(smoothed)` would re-inject           │
│                          │ high-frequency energy and undo the double smoothing, spiking slope changes and false      │
│                          │ reversals.                                                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The edit implements exactly that in three coordinated steps:                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Store the Kalman slope state per step.** The constant-velocity filter already        │
│                          │ maintains `s[1]`, a model-based (inherently denoised) per-sample slope estimate that is   │
│                          │ far cleaner than any finite-difference of the output. Storing it during the forward loop  │
│                          │ costs one array write per iteration — efficiency stays ~1.00.                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Add a short zero-phase box pass over the stored slope.** The raw slope state still   │
│                          │ carries residual jitter; a short odd-length box filter (length ≈ 2·(sg_half//2)+1, i.e. 5 │
│                          │ taps for window_size 20) suppresses it. Because the whole pipeline is batch (the SG stage │
│                          │ is already zero-phase), a *centered* box on the slope adds no phase delay — it only       │
│                          │ slightly under-compensates at true turning points, which is precisely where overshoot     │
│                          │ would create false reversals. Edge handling uses point-reflection padding (2·edge −       │
│                          │ reflected), which extends a locally linear trend correctly at the boundaries instead of   │
│                          │ flattening it, preserving trend fidelity at the array ends where the trimmed output       │
│                          │ actually lives.                                                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. **Apply `lead = 0.4 * sg_half` and emit the compensated signal.** If the smoothed      │
│                          │ estimate approximates the true signal delayed by L samples, adding `lead·slope` advances  │
│                          │ it to an effective lag of L − lead, cutting lag_error while holding tracking accuracy.    │
│                          │ The lead scales with window size, so it generalizes across window_size settings, and the  │
│                          │ compensation is conservative (the causal slope is slightly attenuated), preventing        │
│                          │ over-lead artifacts.                                                                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Net expected effect: lag_error drops below 0.29, responsiveness rises from 0.77, and      │
│                          │ since lag bias is a systematic component of `filtered − clean`, correlation, avg_error    │
│                          │ and noise_reduction all improve; using the denoised slope protects the hard-won           │
│                          │ slope_changes/false_reversals/smoothness gains. Guards for tiny inputs (n ≤ bh+1) and the │
│                          │ existing degenerate-convolution fallback keep success_rate at 1.00.                       │
