│ patch_description        │ The current Kalman filter achieves excellent lag error (0.18) and correlation (0.91), but │
│                          │ suffers from high slope_changes (317.2) and false_reversals (253.6) — the causal filter   │
│                          │ tracks noise-induced micro-oscillations too closely. Following the recommendation, I add  │
│                          │ a final lightweight zero-phase post-processing stage: compute the gradient of the Kalman  │
│                          │ output, flag short segments where the sign of the gradient flips while the gradient       │
│                          │ magnitude is small relative to the median (residual micro-oscillations), and apply an     │
│                          │ extra short zero-phase Savitzky-Golay pass only in a dilated neighborhood of those        │
│                          │ flagged regions. Since smoothing is restricted to flagged neighborhoods, the              │
│                          │ well-performing lag/correlation characteristics remain intact elsewhere, while spurious   │
│                          │ directional reversals are suppressed. This directly targets slope_changes and             │
│                          │ false_reversals — the two weakest metrics — without sacrificing the responsiveness        │
│                          │ advantage over the previous zero-phase-heavy program (which had 0.29 lag but only scored  │
│                          │ 0.59).                                                                                    │
