│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ adaptive_sg_by_local_volatility                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ The best prior program (score 0.59) used a constant-velocity Kalman prefilter followed by │
│                          │ a single fixed zero-phase Savitzky-Golay (SG) pass. The feedback identifies the           │
│                          │ smoothness/responsiveness tradeoff as the active frontier: a fixed SG half-window is      │
│                          │ either too wide (lag, damped turns) or too narrow (residual ripple that still triggers    │
│                          │ false reversals). The signal is non-stationary, so noise level varies strongly across the │
│                          │ record. I replace the current constant-acceleration Kalman + RTS smoother (0.56, which    │
│                          │ regressed vs. 0.59) with the winning architecture and make the SG half-window *locally    │
│                          │ adaptive* using the local-volatility array (`local_std`) already computed in Stage 1. For │
│                          │ each of three half-window scales (h/2, h, 3h/2) I precompute an order-2 SG kernel and     │
│                          │ smooth the Kalman output with all three via one convolution each (all O(n), preserving    │
│                          │ efficiency_score 1.00). Per-sample weights are soft Gaussians in volatility space         │
│                          │ centered at the 20th/50th/80th percentiles of local volatility, normalized across scales. │
│                          │ Volatile regions thus get the wide window (where spurious slope reversals and false       │
│                          │ reversals originate), quiet regions get the narrow window (preserving the 0.74–0.77       │
│                          │ responsiveness and genuine trend dynamics). Soft weighting avoids seams/discontinuities   │
│                          │ that hard block switching would introduce. Zero-phase convolution keeps lag error low;    │
│                          │ order-2 polynomials preserve genuine turning points exactly.                              │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0279                                                                                   │
