│ patch_description        │ The current program's main weakness vs. the best prior generation is lag (0.35 vs 0.28)   │
│                          │ and responsiveness (0.74), while it already wins on slope changes and false reversals     │
│                          │ (66.4). The lag compensation multiplies the smoothed slope by `lead = window_size/8 =     │
│                          │ 2.5`. Although the RTS-smoothed slope channel is already well denoised, residual          │
│                          │ curvature-related ripple in it can still inject jitter when amplified by a larger lead.   │
│                          │ Following the recommendation, I (1) raise the lead factor toward a more aggressive        │
│                          │ compensation (window_size/5 = 4.0), and (2) first apply a short zero-phase SG pass to the │
│                          │ slope channel before the lead multiplication, so the amplified term carries only          │
│                          │ genuinely low-frequency trend information. This should pull lag_error down toward         │
│                          │ ~0.28-0.30 and responsiveness up without reintroducing the false reversals that plagued   │
│                          │ the raw-gradient approach (Gen 3's 92.8 → current 66.4 stays intact because the slope is  │
│                          │ double-denoised: RTS smoother + SG pass).                                                 │
