│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ widen_sg_with_slope_lead_compensation                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The two top performers differ mainly in slope_changes/false_reversals (smoothness) vs     │
│                          │ lag_error. Current program wins on lag (0.28) and noise reduction (0.70) but loses on     │
│                          │ false reversals (84.6 vs 66 in the RTS variants). A cheap way to get both: widen the SG   │
│                          │ half-window from window_size//4 to window_size//3 to further suppress residual ripple     │
│                          │ that drives spurious slope reversals, then compensate the added group delay with a        │
│                          │ first-order trend lead: shift the output forward along its own (already denoised) slope   │
│                          │ channel. Since the slope is taken from the fully smoothed signal, it carries little       │
│                          │ noise, so the lead recovers responsiveness without re-injecting jitter or false           │
│                          │ reversals. The lead factor window_size//6 approximately cancels the extra delay from the  │
│                          │ wider zero-phase window. Alignment and length contract are untouched (final trim stays    │
│                          │ end-aligned, length n - window_size + 1).                                                 │
