│ patch_name               │ denoised_slope_lead_compensation                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ The Kalman filter's lag error (0.26) and slope changes (82.20) can be improved by adding  │
│                          │ lead compensation using the *RTS-smoothed* Kalman slope state rather than a raw gradient. │
│                          │ The smoother already produces a denoised slope estimate (`smoothed[:, 1]`) as part of the │
│                          │ state vector — using it is essentially free. Applying a short 5-tap moving average to     │
│                          │ this slope channel removes any residual high-frequency energy before lead compensation,   │
│                          │ so a moderately aggressive lead (0.3 × effective lag) reduces lag error without           │
│                          │ re-injecting noise or causing spurious reversals. Additionally, tightening the            │
│                          │ reversal-gate thresholds slightly (sig 1.5→1.8, innovation significance 1.0→1.2) reduces  │
│                          │ false reversals (currently 60.40) by demanding more statistical evidence before boosting  │
│                          │ process noise, at minimal cost to genuine turning-point responsiveness since the gate     │
│                          │ only *increases* agility when triggered.                                                  │
