│                          │ `smoothed[:, 1]` for free — it is model-consistent, zero-phase, and carries none of the   │
│                          │ high-frequency energy that made raw `np.gradient`-based lead compensation fail in prior   │
│                          │ generations. Adding a first-order lead term `lead * slope` to the level estimate          │
│                          │ compensates the remaining group delay of the RTS smoother without re-injecting noise. I   │
│                          │ keep the lead modest (0.3 × window_size/4, capped at 2.0) — per the prior feedback,       │
│                          │ aggressive leads only failed because they used noisy slope channels; with the RTS slope   │
│                          │ state a moderate lead is safe. The lead also sharpens turning points, which should reduce │
│                          │ the lag error while the downstream localized hysteresis stage continues to suppress any   │
│                          │ residual slope-induced micro-oscillations (and now acts on the lead-compensated signal,   │
│                          │ which is the correct order).                                                              │
