│ patch_name               │ widen_reversal_gate_hysteresis                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ The current bottleneck is slope changes (82.2) and false reversals (60.4). The Kalman's   │
│                          │ reversal gate is the analog of the "second pass" window: its comparison horizon           │
│                          │ (reversal_window = window_size // 4) is the least-tuned parameter and is too narrow — a   │
│                          │ short slope-history lookback makes the gate trigger off noise-induced slope sign flickers │
│                          │ rather than genuine turning points. Two coordinated changes: (1) widen the lookback to    │
│                          │ window_size // 3, so the gate compares against a more statistically stable slope          │
│                          │ estimate; (2) add sign-hysteresis — only fire the process-noise boost when the *new*      │
│                          │ slope has been sign-consistent for two consecutive steps AND the sign change is           │
│                          │ significant, which suppresses single-sample noise spikes from inflating process noise and │
│                          │ causing lag/drift. Together these reduce spurious reversals (helping both slope_changes   │
│                          │ and false_reversals) while preserving the genuine-turning-point responsiveness via the    │
│                          │ retained significance test (sig > 1.5, inno_sig > 1.0).                                   │
