                        Patch Metadata - Gen 39/100 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ rts_dualsg_gated_lead                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The current best program (0.65) wins via a 2-state  Kalman forward pass + standard RTS    │
│                          │ backward recursion + volatility-blended multi-scale zero-phase SG. The recommendation     │
│                          │ notes that the only remaining gaps are smoothness/slope-reversals and lag error, and that │
│                          │ prior generations lost points through *unconditional* slope-lead compensation (jitter in  │
│                          │ quiet regions).                                                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This design keeps the two proven cores (2-state RTS with Q = 0.05·(median|diff|)²,        │
│                          │ volatility-adaptive SG) and adds two novel, targeted mechanisms:                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Dual-cascade SG polish**: after the adaptive multi-scale SG pass, a second short     │
│                          │ fixed half-width (window/8) zero-phase SG pass is cascaded. Cascading two short           │
│                          │ zero-phase kernels suppresses the residual high-frequency ripple that drives spurious     │
│                          │ slope reversals far more efficiently per unit of trend distortion than a single wide      │
│                          │ kernel — the 3-state/reversal-gate approaches that injected jitter are avoided entirely.  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Confidence-gated trend lead**: instead of adding lead·slope everywhere (which hurt   │
│                          │ smoothness in prior gens), the denoised slope channel is polished with a 5-tap SG kernel  │
│                          │ and compared against its own robust noise scale (MAD). A soft tanh gate scales the lead   │
│                          │ so that compensation is applied only where the slope is statistically significant —       │
│                          │ genuine trends get lag compensation, flat noisy regions get none, so no jitter is         │
│                          │ injected.                                                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The result combines full bidirectional evidence (RTS), multi-scale smoothing, and lag     │
│                          │ compensation that is *conditional on trend confidence* — a fundamentally different lead   │
│                          │ mechanism than the failed unconditional/hysteresis variants.                              │
