│ patch_name               │ wide_sg_scale_bank                                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The current program's reversal-suppression bottleneck (47.6 slope changes, 34.0 false     │
│                          │ reversals) is governed by the width of the widest Savitzky-Golay kernel, which is the     │
│                          │ least-tuned parameter. This variant widens the widest SG half-width from `3·sg_half//2`   │
│                          │ (effectively capped near `sg_half`) up to `2·sg_half`, while retaining the short scale at │
│                          │ `sg_half//2` and the mid scale at `sg_half`. The three-scale zero-phase SG bank (widest → │
│                          │ mid → short) applies progressively finer oscillation suppression: the widest quadratic    │
│                          │ kernel kills residual noise-induced direction reversals in volatile regions, the mid      │
│                          │ kernel preserves genuine curvature, and the short kernel finishes micro-ripple removal    │
│                          │ without adding lag (all passes are zero-phase with symmetric edge padding). The gated     │
│                          │ RTS/gradient slope-lead compensation is retained but recomputed after the widened bank so │
│                          │ responsiveness is maintained (the gate keeps the lead from re-injecting noise). Output is │
│                          │ end-aligned and validated to length `n - window_size + 1`; no walrus operators appear in  │
│                          │ slices.                                                                                   │
