│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ tighten_hysteresis_params                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current best (combined 0.63) is a plateau point where the localized hysteresis stage  │
│                          │ already delivered a measurable gain (slope_changes 82.2→79.6, false_reversals 60.4→58.4)  │
│                          │ without hurting lag (0.26) or accuracy (0.97). The remaining gap between slope_changes    │
│                          │ (79.6) and false_reversals (58.4) indicates residual micro-oscillations that the          │
│                          │ hysteresis flags but only partially smooths, because the "weak gradient" threshold k=0.5  │
│                          │ relative to the median gradient excludes borderline flips that still count as slope       │
│                          │ changes in the metric.                                                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Following the minimal-mutation discipline (one attributable change, no composite edits),  │
│                          │ I propose tightening only the hysteresis detection parameters: (1) raise flip_span from 3 │
│                          │ to 4 so sign flips up to 4 samples apart are also treated as micro-oscillation            │
│                          │ neighborhoods, and (2) lower k from 0.5 to 0.35 so more borderline-weak gradient flips    │
│                          │ are flagged for local SG smoothing. Both parameters only affect which neighborhoods get   │
│                          │ the tiny zero-phase SG pass; the Kalman/RTS core, gate constants, and output contract     │
│                          │ remain untouched, so any score delta is attributable to the detection tuning alone. Since │
│                          │ the SG pass is zero-phase (symmetric 5-tap), widening its application does not increase   │
│                          │ lag error or phase delay — it directly targets the slope-change and false-reversal        │
│                          │ objectives while preserving responsiveness and tracking accuracy.                         │
