│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ use_rts_slope_in_lead_stage                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The last identified noise leak in the pipeline is Stage 5's slope channel: it is derived  │
│                          │ from `np.gradient(smoothed)`, i.e., a finite difference of an already-smoothed signal.    │
│                          │ Finite differencing amplifies any residual ripple left by the SG cascade, so the sigmoid  │
│                          │ gate (`gate = 1/(1+exp(-(|slopes|/(2*mad_sl) - 2)))` opens on noise-induced slope spikes, │
│                          │ injecting lead into quiet regions and causing spurious directional reversals              │
│                          │ (false_reversals 51, slope_changes 69). Meanwhile the RTS smoother already produces an    │
│                          │ optimal, low-noise, zero-phase slope estimate `sm[:, 1]` (the smoothed slope state),      │
│                          │ which is discarded by the current code. This is exactly the "gradient vs. RTS-slope"      │
│                          │ blend issue flagged in prior generations: pure RTS-slope historically held lag at         │
│                          │ 0.25-0.40 with best-in-class reversals. As a minimal, single-attribution mutation I       │
│                          │ replace only the slope source in Stage 5 with `sm[:, 1]`, keeping the 5-tap slope polish, │
│                          │ the MAD noise scale, the sigmoid gate constants, and `lead = max(1, window_size/8)`       │
│                          │ exactly as-is, so any score delta is attributable to the slope-source swap alone. The RTS │
│                          │ slope is smoother (fewer gate openings on noise -> fewer false reversals and slope        │
│                          │ changes) yet is a genuine smoothed trend estimate (trend tracking / lag preserved),       │
│                          │ directly improving the reversal and slope-change objectives that dominate the combined    │
│                          │ score.                                                                                    │
