│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ add_reversal_gate_and_hysteresis                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ The current program's strengths are low lag (0.28) and low slope changes (66), but its    │
│                          │ false_reversals (49.2) and smoothness (0.23) trail the best prior variants, which used    │
│                          │ two mechanisms the current pipeline lacks: (1) a trend-reversal gate that temporarily     │
│                          │ boosts the Kalman process noise at statistically significant turning points, so the       │
│                          │ filter re-locks onto genuine reversals quickly instead of dithering through them, and (2) │
│                          │ a final localized reversal-suppression hysteresis pass that smooths only neighborhoods    │
│                          │ with weak, rapidly-flipping gradients (residual micro-oscillations) — a targeted way to   │
│                          │ cut spurious slope sign changes without adding global lag.                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This is a crossover: keep the current multi-scale SG stage and slope-lead compensation    │
│                          │ untouched (they deliver the current lag/responsiveness), and graft on the two             │
│                          │ reversal-suppression mechanisms. The gate uses the filter's own slope-state covariance to │
│                          │ judge significance (slope change > 1.5 sigma of slope uncertainty AND a significant       │
│                          │ innovation), so it fires only on genuine turning points — boosting Q there actually       │
│                          │ *reduces* lag error at reversals. The hysteresis pass is restricted to weak,              │
│                          │ flipping-gradient neighborhoods (dilated by 2 samples), so genuine trend dynamics         │
│                          │ elsewhere are preserved; its SG kernel is zero-phase, adding no phase delay. Expected     │
│                          │ effect: fewer slope_changes and false_reversals with lag unchanged.                       │
