│ patch_name               │ adaptive_kalman_volblend_sg_cascade                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ Fundamentally different internal pipeline: a robust constant-velocity Kalman + RTS        │
│                          │ smoother for the trend estimate, followed by a *volatility-blended two-kernel zero-phase  │
│                          │ SG cascade* instead of fixed multi-pass smoothing. Two precomputed SG kernels (narrow     │
│                          │ `h1` and wide `h2`) are blended per-sample with a sigmoid weight of local volatility      │
│                          │ (smoothed |diff|): in quiet regions the output is dominated by the narrow kernel          │
│                          │ (responsiveness, low lag), while in volatile regions the wide kernel takes over           │
│                          │ (suppressing noise-induced slope reversals and false reversals). A final light zero-phase │
│                          │ polish pass removes residual ripple, and a mild slope-lead term restores corner dynamics  │
│                          │ lost to wide-kernel attenuation. All stages are O(n) convolutions plus the O(n) Kalman    │
│                          │ recursion, keeping execution time near the previous best. The adaptive blend directly     │
│                          │ targets the smoothness/false-reversal bottleneck (weakest metrics at 0.29/0.24) without   │
│                          │ sacrificing the strong correlation (0.95+) and lag (0.27) already achieved, because       │
│                          │ kernel widening happens only where the signal is genuinely volatile.                      │
