│ patch_name               │ kalman_rts_multiscale_sg_hybrid                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ Crossover of the two implementations. Parent A (prior program) achieves far fewer slope   │
│                          │ changes (48 vs 64) and fewer false reversals (35 vs 45) via a double zero-phase SG pass   │
│                          │ and a conservative slope-lead compensation, but has higher lag (0.35) and lower noise     │
│                          │ reduction (0.65). Parent B (current) achieves better lag (0.27), noise reduction (0.82)   │
│                          │ and correlation (0.97) via volatility-gated multi-scale SG. The hybrid keeps the shared   │
│                          │ Kalman+RTS core, then applies: (1) the volatility-gated multi-scale zero-phase SG from    │
│                          │ parent B (strong smoothing in volatile regions, preserving dynamics in quiet regions),    │
│                          │ (2) a short second zero-phase SG pass from parent A to further suppress spurious          │
│                          │ reversals where it matters most, and (3) a mild, conservative slope-lead compensation     │
│                          │ (scaled-down version of parent A's stage 4) to restore corner responsiveness, reducing    │
│                          │ lag without reintroducing noise. Output is end-aligned and trimmed to sliding-window      │
│                          │ length, same as both parents. All stages are O(n) convolutions; the heavy state-space     │
│                          │ loops are shared by both parents and unchanged.                                           │
