│                          │ saturated at 0.97. The current slope-lead compensation only advances the level by a       │
│                          │ first-order term (lead_gain * sg_half * slope). Because the signal uses a                 │
│                          │ constant-acceleration model and the smoother already estimates curvature (state[2]), we   │
│                          │ can compensate the group delay more accurately with the second-order Taylor term: y(t+d)  │
│                          │ ≈ y(t) + d·slope + ½d²·curvature. Using the already-computed smoothed curvature channel   │
│                          │ costs nothing extra and reduces lag on curved segments (peaks/troughs) precisely where    │
│                          │ lag error is largest. To keep false reversals in check, the curvature term is damped      │
│                          │ (factor 0.25 of its nominal coefficient) and lead_gain is raised modestly from 0.2 to 0.3 │
│                          │ — the curvature correction makes the higher lead safe because it no longer overshoots on  │
│                          │ curved trajectories. This should lower lag_error without increasing slope_changes or      │
│                          │ false_reversals, improving the multi-objective composite score.                           │
