│ patch_description        │ The current program's main weakness versus prior generations is slope_changes (64.2) and  │
│                          │ false_reversals (45.0). Prior program 3 achieved 48.4/34.8 using two cheap post-stages:   │
│                          │ (1) conservative slope-lead compensation, which adds a fraction of the                    │
│                          │ zero-phase-estimated slope back to the signal to restore corner dynamics lost to the      │
│                          │ multi-scale SG attenuation (improving responsiveness and tracking without adding lag,     │
│                          │ since the SG is zero-phase), and (2) a light short-window zero-phase SG polish pass that  │
│                          │ removes residual ripple. Both are O(n) convolution steps and risk negligible lag. I add   │
│                          │ these two stages between the volatility-gated blend and the end-aligned trim, using the   │
│                          │ same `_sg_smooth` helper already defined and the existing narrow half-width (`sg_half //  │
│                          │ 2`) so the polish matches the medium scale. The lead factor is conservative (0.5 *        │
│                          │ sg_half * 0.25) exactly matching the best prior performing variant.                       │
