├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ slope_conditional_quiet_region_polish                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ Gens 82–85 established that extra unconditional SG passes cannot reduce the ~48 slope     │
│                          │ changes / ~35 false reversals — they attenuate corners and hurt lag. The residual         │
│                          │ reversals occur almost entirely in quiet (low-slope) regions where the output still       │
│                          │ dithers. So instead of widening any global pass, I add a *slope-magnitude-conditional*    │
│                          │ polish: compute the slope of the current output, and only where |slope| is below a        │
│                          │ volatility-scaled threshold (0.25 * q_scale, using the Kalman stage's robust              │
│                          │ diff-median), apply a very short zero-phase SG (half-width max(2, sg_half//3)). Where the │
│                          │ slope is significant (genuine trends, transitions), the output is passed through          │
│                          │ untouched, preserving responsiveness. The blend is done via np.where with an explicitly   │
│                          │ length-checked branch (guarding against Gen 90's length-mismatch crash: if the short      │
│                          │ kernel cannot be applied to this length, skip the polish entirely). This attacks the      │
│                          │ slope-change and false-reversal bottlenecks without degrading lag_error or                │
│                          │ responsiveness, since quiet-region smoothing is zero-phase and short-windowed.            │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0241                                                                                   │
