│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ lead_compensation_curvature_damper                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The remaining bottleneck is residual slope oscillation: slope_changes (63.2) and          │
│                          │ false_reversals (45.8) dominate the composite score while correlation (0.96),             │
│                          │ noise_reduction (0.78), and efficiency (1.00) are near-saturated. The Stage 3 lead        │
│                          │ compensation injects a first-difference term that amplifies curvature spikes at turning   │
│                          │ points, which is exactly what produces spurious directional reversals. Adding a curvature │
│                          │ (second-difference) damper directly penalizes those oscillation spikes without adding     │
│                          │ lag, since np.gradient is a symmetric (zero-phase) operator. To avoid noise-induced       │
│                          │ over-damping, the curvature is clipped robustly at ±3·median(|d2|). The coefficient is    │
│                          │ scaled by sg_half so the damping strength scales with the smoothing window (consistent    │
│                          │ with Stage 3's lead scaling). This targets the false-reversal and slope-change metrics    │
│                          │ while leaving the lag error and tracking accuracy essentially untouched (the damper is    │
│                          │ zero at inflection-free regions).                                                         │
